        "\n",
        "This interactive is deliberately idealized.  Real embeddings are high-dimensional, noisy, and trained from data.  Nevertheless, the same vector operation and cosine nearest-neighbor rule are used.\n",
        "\n",
        "# Worked example: PPMI by hand\n",
        "\n",
        "Suppose a tiny corpus produces the following co-occurrence counts:\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "C=\n",
        "\\begin{bmatrix}\n",
        "10 & 2\\\\\n",
        "1 & 7\n",
        "\\end{bmatrix}.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The total count is\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "N=10+2+1+7=20.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "For entry $(1,1)$,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(W=1,C=1)=\\frac{10}{20}=0.5,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "while\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(W=1)=\\frac{12}{20}=0.6,\n",
        "\\qquad\n",
        "P(C=1)=\\frac{11}{20}=0.55.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Thus\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{PMI}(1,1)\n",
        "=\\log\\frac{0.5}{0.6\\cdot 0.55}\n",
        "=\\log(1.515\\ldots)>0.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "The pair $(1,1)$ occurs more often than expected under independence.\n",
        "\n",
        "For entry $(1,2)$,\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(W=1,C=2)=\\frac{2}{20}=0.1,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "while\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "P(W=1)P(C=2)=\\frac{12}{20}\\cdot\\frac{9}{20}=0.27.\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "Therefore\n",
        "\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\\operatorname{PMI}(1,2)=\\log\\frac{0.1}{0.27}<0,\n",
        "$$\n",
        "\n",
        "and the PPMI value is set to zero.\n",
        "\n",
        "# AI-assisted learning components\n",
        "\n",
        "AI tools can help students explore word embeddings, but they should be used as mathematical assistants rather than authorities.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Prompt 1: explain an embedding method mathematically\n",
        "\n",
        "Use an AI assistant to answer:\n",
        "\n",
        "> Explain the skip-gram model as a maximum likelihood problem.  Define the vocabulary, the input embedding matrix, the output embedding matrix, the softmax probability, and the negative log-likelihood.  Avoid implementation details until the mathematical objective is clear.\n",
        "\n",
        "Then check whether the answer includes:\n",
        "\n",
        "- a conditional probability $P(c\\mid w)$;\n",
        "- inner products between word and context vectors;\n",
        "- a softmax denominator over the vocabulary;\n",
        "- a sum over observed word-context pairs;\n",
        "- a clear distinction between input and output embeddings.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Prompt 2: audit a nearest-neighbor result\n",
        "\n",
        "Ask:\n",
        "\n",
        "> I trained word embeddings and found the nearest neighbors of the word \"model\".  How can I tell whether the neighbors reflect semantics, syntax, frequency effects, or corpus bias?  Give a mathematical checklist.\n",
        "\n",
        "A good answer should mention cosine similarity, vector norms, word frequency, training corpus, context window, and the difference between association and truth.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Prompt 3: derive the gradient\n",
        "\n",
        "Ask:\n",
        "\n",
        "> For the skip-gram softmax loss $\\ell(w,c)=-\\log P(c\\mid w)$, derive $\\partial \\ell/\\partial v_w$ and explain the sign of the update.\n",
        "\n",
        "Then compare the result with the gradient derived earlier in this chapter.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Prompt 4: detect hidden assumptions\n",
        "\n",
        "Ask:\n",
        "\n",
        "> What assumptions are hidden in the distributional hypothesis?  Give examples where words appear in similar contexts but should not be considered semantically equivalent.\n",
        "\n",
        "This encourages students to connect mathematical modeling assumptions with linguistic and social limitations.\n",
        "\n",
        "# Exercises\n",
        "\n",
        "## Conceptual exercises\n",
        "\n",
        "1. Explain why one-hot vectors do not encode semantic similarity.\n",
        "2. What information is contained in a row of a co-occurrence matrix?\n",
        "3. Why does PMI correct for word frequency better than raw co-occurrence counts?\n",
        "4. What is the difference between a word embedding matrix and a context embedding matrix?\n",
        "5. In what sense is GloVe a matrix approximation method?\n",
        "6. Why can static word embeddings struggle with polysemy?\n",
        "7. Why might cosine similarity be preferred over Euclidean distance for word embeddings?\n",
        "8. What does it mean for an analogy to be represented by a linear direction?\n",
        "\n",
